CRASH-PREFIX RESIDUALS:
CRASH-PREFIX mirror-fetch-migration.1: no etude fetch refspec is CONFIGURED; push
config is whatever it already was. The precise claim is "no NEW fetch can be
started with the legacy refspec after this step", NOT "run refs are not prunable":
a `git fetch --prune` already in flight read its refspecs at startup and still
holds the legacy one. Git's config lock orders writes, not readers that have
already read. That window is one-time and INHERITED — etude-i19 already removes
this refspec and has the identical race — and no ordering at any layer closes it,
because the hazardous state is in another process's memory. Post-etude-nad it is
a LOUD FAILURE rather than silent loss: a vanished run ref makes the next capture
refuse instead of rebuilding a fresh manifest.
CRASH-PREFIX mirror-fetch-migration.2: as .1, plus the canonical push refspec
present. Sharing works; no fetch refspec exists, so no mirror exists. Safe.
CRASH-PREFIX mirror-fetch-migration.3: as .2, plus runs mirrored on fetch. Retros
and evals simply not mirrored yet. No push refspec can match the mirror, so it
cannot leave this clone. Incomplete mirroring, no data loss, no upload path.
CRASH-PREFIX mirror-fetch-migration.4: as .3, plus retros mirrored.
CRASH-PREFIX mirror-fetch-migration.5: fully migrated.

The ordering rule is etude-i19's, not this layout's: the legacy hazardous fetch
refspec is removed FIRST, so the earliest prefix is the safest state rather than
the most exposed one.

TRUST BOUNDARY: none crossed. `etude init` runs in the operator's own repository
under their own account. The only caller-supplied input reaching a ref path is
the `--remote` name, which is validated by CONSTRUCTING the mirror ref and
checking it with `git check-ref-format`.

## Behaviour-preserving invariants (confirm, don't reconstruct)

1. `refs/etude-mirror/` is a SIBLING of `refs/etude/`, so no refspec spanning
   `refs/etude/*` can match it. Refspec globbing and `for-each-ref` match on path
   components, and `-` is not `/`.
2. As RAW STRINGS, `"refs/etude-mirror/…"` DOES start with `"refs/etude"`. Every
   production constant is slash-anchored (`refs/etude/`), which is what keeps (1)
   true for prefix tests too. Audited: no unanchored `HasPrefix(x, "refs/etude")`
   exists in production Go. `TestMirrorNamespaceIsDisjointFromLocal` pins the
   invariant AND deliberately asserts the raw-string overlap still exists, so the
   warning comments cannot be "simplified" away.
3. Forced (`+`) applies ONLY to mirror destinations. The local namespace is never
   a fetch destination in configured refspecs, so no forced refspec can overwrite
   an authoritative local run.
4. `ValidateMirrorRemote` builds a CONCRETE probe ref (`…/runs/probe`), never a
   `*` glob, before `check-ref-format` — `*` is illegal in a plain ref name and
   would otherwise reject every remote and give the right answer for the wrong
   reason. This mirrors git's own `valid_remote_name`, which validates by
   building `refs/remotes/<name>/test`.
5. Both refspec lists are driven from `refstore.Kinds`, so a new kind cannot be
   silently omitted from one side.

## Verification evidence

Eleven new tests. The load-bearing ones:
- `TestFetchPruneOnlyDeletesFromMirror` — the acceptance test. Real bare remote,
  real `git fetch --prune`, both halves of the asymmetry asserted: a
  locally-produced run the remote has never seen SURVIVES, and a mirror entry
  whose source was deleted on the remote IS pruned, with the local run still
  intact after the second prune.
- `TestInitDoesNotRemoveItsOwnMirroredFetchRefspec` — second init is a no-op;
  this is the self-destruct the rejected nested layout would have caused.
- `TestInitStillRemovesLegacyBroadFetchRefspecAfterMirroring` — the literal i19
  hazard is still removed, the mirrored refspecs installed, and the remote's
  `refs/heads/*` refspec untouched.
- `TestInitLeavesPushRefspecUntouched` — including a user's deliberate
  `refs/etude/*:refs/archive/etude/*` variant.
- `TestSyncFetchNeverPrunes` / `TestSyncFetchIntoLocalNamespaceIsNotForced` —
  guards on code this diff does NOT change. sync's refspec targets the
  AUTHORITATIVE namespace and is not in config, so if it ever gained `--prune` or
  a `+` it would re-arm the etude-nad data-loss mode through a path i19's
  config-side fix does not cover.
- `TestBroadPushRefspecDoesNotCarryMirrorToRemote` — settles the push question by
  DEMONSTRATION rather than assertion: a real push to a real bare remote, with the
  broad refspec deliberately left in place, carries the local run and does NOT
  carry the mirror. Under the rejected nested layout this test would fail.
- `TestConfigWrittenRemoteBypassesGitNameValidation` — pins why etude cannot
  delegate remote-name validation to git: `git remote add` refuses the name, but a
  config-written remote is visible to `git remote get-url`, so it reaches etude.
- `TestMirrorNamespaceIsDisjointFromLocal`, `TestRefstoreRejectsWritesToMirrorNamespace`,
  `TestListLocalPrefixesExcludeMirrorRefs`, `TestInitInstallsNoMirrorRefspecForIllegalRemoteName`.

BUILT-BINARY PROOF against a real bare remote:
```
removed  remote.origin.fetch = +refs/etude/*:refs/etude/*
configured remote.origin.fetch = +refs/etude/runs/*:refs/etude-mirror/origin/runs/*   (+retros, +evals)
